id: soft-environment
name: Soft Environment
kind: battlefield
environment: true
hardnessMin: 0
hardnessMax: 0
collisionCondition: prone
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Deep sand or snow. Hardness Rank 0, so hitting it barely hurts - and it puts you on
  your back, which is the point.
text: >
  Soft Environment: An environment where the ground is soft and pliable, making it
  difficult to quickly get back up. Often used to represent an environment with deep
  sand or snow.

  Hardness Rank: 0

  Effects: If a Character collides with a Square of this Battle Environment, they are
  knocked Prone.

---

# The first Environment whose Hardness Rank is not a range. "Hardness Rank: 0" and nothing
# else, so the ARC has nothing to pick and the row says so by offering one thing.
#
# Rank 0 is 1(bT) rather than nothing - "Features cannot have a Hardness value of 0, but
# the Squares themselves can", and a Square is exactly what this is. So landing in deep
# snow costs a point a base Tier and leaves you on your back, which is the trade: it barely
# hurts and it is the hardest ground in the game to fight from.
#
# --- Knocked Prone ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If a Character collides with a Square of this Battle Environment, they are knocked
# Prone."
#
# A Square of this Environment, which is the ground - not a Feature standing on it. Those
# are two different collisions and the rules have always told them apart: Sudden Stop names
# "Feature Collision or Ground Collision respectively". The collision window knew the
# difference and only hinted at it, labelling whichever Rank matched the ground; it asks
# outright now, and this is what made the difference worth asking about.
#
# `collisionCondition` is the header that carries it. A Condition by name rather than a
# script, because the collision is an event - a window somebody fills in - and there is no
# value here for a passive to write.
#
# No duration. The entry gives none, so Prone comes off the way Prone always comes off:
# by getting up.
#
# --- Effects, otherwise ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# None. No statements below the line, so the registry drops the program - the Environment
# does nothing to a character standing in it, only to one arriving at speed.
